-- SUM-TYPE-FIELDS-SPEC §3.2, RULING of 2026-09-13. A partial field read in a
-- LATER CLAUSE of a multi-clause `DECIDE`/`MEANS` group is REFUSED OUTRIGHT. It
-- is never narrowed by the clauses above it.
--
-- There is no green side. This file used to be paired with
-- `ok/sum-fields/fallthrough.l4`, which demonstrated the clause narrowing
-- working; that rule is cut and that file is gone. Everything here is a
-- refusal, and case 11 is the one that says what the ruling COST.
--
-- WHY CUT IT. Three rounds of work on the clause narrowing produced,
-- cumulatively: two permissive holes that killed programs at run time, a
-- hygiene hole, four false-positive refusals, and two diagnostics that stated
-- the opposite of the truth — including one that told a drafter "nothing here
-- narrows `a`" about a program whose clause 1 visibly matched a constructor.
-- §3.2's test of an S3 rule is that its diagnostic must EXPLAIN the narrowing;
-- a rule that cannot be explained is too clever and is cut. A refusal teaches
-- itself at the moment of failure, and magic that ACCEPTS is the expensive
-- kind, because when it is wrong nobody sees it.
--
-- WHAT THE DRAFTER WRITES INSTEAD is a `CONSIDER`, which does narrow — and it
-- is what every message below recommends. `ok/sum-fields/narrowing.l4` is the
-- green file for all four narrowing forms.
--
-- WHAT THE CASES ARE FOR. Cases 2, 3 and 4 are shapes no narrowing of any kind
-- could have covered: a selector used as a value has no base at all, a base
-- written as some other constructor is already as narrow as it gets, and a
-- nested helper's own parameter is not a column of the group. Case 5 checks
-- that being in a later clause does not silence a `CONSIDER` the drafter DID
-- write. Cases 1, 6, 7, 8, 9, 15 were refusals before the ruling too, and their
-- messages change. Cases 10-14 were ACCEPTED before it, and they are what the
-- ruling actually decided:
--
--   10 is the wildcard spelling, where the read is on the column binder itself
--      rather than on a catch-all's fresh one.
--   11 is the accepted cost — a program that is provably total and is refused
--      anyway. Do not "fix" it; it is the trade.
--   12 is a permissive hole the cut CLOSES: this program used to check clean
--      and then die at run time (it was `ok/sum-fields/partial-selector-runtime.l4`).
--   13 is a false-positive refusal the cut ALSO closes — round 3 refused it for
--      a reason that was not true; it is refused here for the honest one.
--   14 checks that the third clause of a group, nested two fall-throughs deep,
--      gets the same verdict and the same sentence.
--
-- (No IMPORT: this file's golden would otherwise carry an import-resolution
-- log, and `apply` below is all that prelude was wanted for.)

DECLARE Actor IS ONE OF
    Landlord
    Agent
    Tenant   HAS monthly_rent IS A NUMBER

-- 1. THE GROUP'S OWN COLUMN. The flagship shape, and the one Meng was shown.
--    `Landlord` is matched by clause 1 and `Agent` is not matched at all, so
--    even a rule that reasoned about clause order had to refuse this. What
--    changed is the SENTENCE: it now names the rule as the cause instead of
--    naming a clause, because there is no per-clause analysis left to name one.
GIVEN s IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE rent Landlord IS 0
DECIDE rent s        IS s's monthly_rent

-- 2. THE SELECTOR AS A VALUE, with no base at all. There is nothing for a
--    clause to have narrowed, so being in a later clause changes nothing.
GIVEN f IS A FUNCTION FROM Actor TO NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`apply` f MEANS f Agent

GIVEN s IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE asValue Landlord IS 0
DECIDE asValue s        IS `apply` monthly_rent

-- 3. A BASE WRITTEN AS ANOTHER CONSTRUCTOR. `theLandlord` is a `Landlord`
--    however the surrounding clauses fell out, so the read is simply wrong
--    about which constructor it reads from.
GIVEN s IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE constructed Landlord IS 0
DECIDE constructed s        IS theLandlord's monthly_rent
    WHERE
        theLandlord MEANS Landlord

-- 4. A NESTED HELPER'S OWN PARAMETER. `x` is bound by the helper's GIVEN, not
--    by the clause group, so it stands at the whole type no matter where the
--    helper is written.
--
--    THE ONE IMPRECISION IN THE MESSAGE, stated rather than engineered away:
--    the helper's body is lexically inside clause 2, so this read draws the
--    later-clause sentence even though `x` is not a column of anything. The
--    sentence survives that, because it is phrased about the CLAUSE and not
--    about the binder being a column — and it is true here: this IS a later
--    clause, and it does not narrow `x`. Telling the two apart would mean
--    keeping a set of the enclosing group's column binders, which is the
--    clause bookkeeping the ruling cut.
GIVEN s IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE viaHelper Landlord IS 0
DECIDE viaHelper s        IS helper s
    WHERE
        GIVEN x IS AN Actor
        helper x MEANS x's monthly_rent

-- 5. NARROWED TO THE WRONG ARM, inside a later clause. `a` stands at the whole
--    type here; the `WHEN Agent` then narrows it to `Agent`, which has no
--    `monthly_rent`. The point of the case is that the message comes from the
--    `CONSIDER` the drafter DID write, not from the clause it sits in — being
--    in a later clause neither silences a narrowing nor supplies one.
GIVEN s IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE wrongArm Landlord IS 0
DECIDE wrongArm a        IS
    CONSIDER a
    WHEN Agent THEN a's monthly_rent
    OTHERWISE       0

-- 6. TWO COLUMNS, AND THE EARLIER CLAUSE TESTS BOTH. `dueFrom FALSE Landlord`
--    does not match clause 1, so clause 2 really does run with `a = Landlord`:
--    this program is UNSAFE, and was refused before the ruling as well as
--    after. Widening the first column to the wildcard spelling makes it safe —
--    and it is refused anyway now, which is case 11.
GIVEN flag IS A BOOLEAN
      s    IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE dueFrom TRUE Landlord IS 0
DECIDE dueFrom flag a        IS a's monthly_rent

-- 7. A CLAUSE THAT BINDS THE PAYLOAD REFUTABLY. `Tenant 1500` matches only the
--    `Tenant`s whose rent is 1500, so `Landlord` and `Agent` reach clause 2 and
--    the program is unsafe on its own terms. The irrefutable spelling
--    (`DECIDE refutable (Tenant r) IS …`) is safe and is refused too — that is
--    the ruling, not an accident of this pattern.
GIVEN s IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE refutable (Tenant 1500) IS 1
DECIDE refutable a             IS a's monthly_rent

-- 8. THE COLUMN'S TYPE WRITTEN AS A SYNONYM. The message must still name the
--    UNDERLYING type's constructors, which means the selector's domain head has
--    to be seen through `Occupant`. A synonym that defeated that reading would
--    show up as silence, not as a worse message — S2 records nothing for a
--    domain it cannot enumerate — so this case is here to keep the refusal
--    honest rather than to test the synonym for its own sake.
DECLARE Occupant IS Actor

GIVEN o IS AN Occupant
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE synRent Landlord IS 0
DECIDE synRent o        IS o's monthly_rent

-- 9. A DECLARATION SPELLED LIKE THE DESUGARER'S OWN FALL-THROUGH BINDING. The
--    parser names the compiled tail of a clause group `__pm_fallthrough_k`, and
--    a module the printer has re-emitted comes back through the front end
--    carrying that name, so the checker cannot simply reject the spelling: a
--    re-parsed fall-through has no clause matrix to consult and its reads must
--    stay accepted. The provenance test therefore also requires the NULLARY
--    appform the desugarer always builds. Written with an argument, as here,
--    this is an ordinary user declaration and its read is checked like any
--    other — before, the name alone switched the whole check off and the
--    program died at run time.
--
--    The NULLARY spelling — the one form the desugarer itself emits, and so
--    the one form that cannot be excluded — used to switch S2 off as well, and
--    `DECIDE outer a IS `__pm_fallthrough_0` WHERE `__pm_fallthrough_0` MEANS
--    a's monthly_rent` checked clean and died at run time. That half closed for
--    free when the clause narrowing was cut: there is no S2 suppression left
--    anywhere, so no spelling of this name can switch one off (measured
--    2026-09-13). The arity test still earns its place, because the predicate
--    also drives the non-exhaustive-CONSIDER silence. Neither spelling has a
--    fixture in the corpus beyond this one, because pinning the nullary form
--    would mean shipping that name in a file the printer round-trips.
GIVEN s IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `__pm_fallthrough_0` s IS s's monthly_rent

-- 10. THE COLUMN'S OWN NAME, rather than a fresh binder. Reusing the `GIVEN`
--     name is the language's wildcard idiom: the desugarer emits no test and no
--     rebinding, so the read is on the column binder itself rather than on a
--     catch-all's fresh one. Same verdict, and the message names the same
--     binder the drafter wrote.
GIVEN p IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE rentAgain Landlord IS 0
DECIDE rentAgain p        IS p's monthly_rent

-- 11. ★ THE ACCEPTED COST. `Solo` has exactly two constructors, clause 1 takes
--     the one without the field, and nothing else can reach clause 2 — this
--     program is provably total and it evaluated correctly before the ruling.
--     It is refused anyway. That is the trade Meng took, deliberately and with
--     this exact program in front of him: a refusal teaches itself at the
--     moment of failure, and a rule that ACCEPTS is the expensive kind when it
--     is wrong. DO NOT "FIX" THIS CASE. The repair the message offers —
--     rewrite the group as one `CONSIDER` — is what a drafter writes instead.
DECLARE Solo IS ONE OF
    Freeholder
    Leaseholder HAS ground_rent IS A NUMBER

GIVEN z IS A Solo
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE soloRent Freeholder IS 0
DECIDE soloRent z          IS z's ground_rent

-- 12. AN UNTYPED `GIVEN` COLUMN. This is the permissive hole the cut CLOSES,
--     and it is the reason the cut is not merely a deletion: `s` has no
--     declared type, so the rule that was cut could not enumerate its column
--     before the body was checked and fell back to SUPPRESSING the read. The
--     program checked clean and then died at run time on `rent Agent` with
--     "The value Agent has no `monthly_rent` field" — it lived under `ok/` as
--     `sum-fields/partial-selector-runtime.l4` precisely because it did.
--     With no suppression left, the read reaches the ordinary S2 clamp at the
--     READ, where the selector's own domain type is instantiated, and is
--     refused. (Measured 2026-09-13: after the cut, no `.l4` in the tree that
--     type-checks dies this way.)
GIVEN s2
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE untypedRent Landlord IS 0
DECIDE untypedRent s2       IS s2's monthly_rent

-- 13. A DON'T-CARE COLUMN THE CHECKER CANNOT ENUMERATE. `m` is a name no
--     constructor answers to, so the desugarer compiles it to no test at all
--     and clause 1 fires for every number paired with a `Landlord` — which is
--     what makes the second column's `Landlord` genuinely used up. Round 3 of
--     the clause narrowing REFUSED this program for a reason that was not
--     true, and refused it while accepting the identical shape over a
--     `BOOLEAN`; renaming `m` to `n` also changed the answer. It is refused
--     here too, and the difference is the whole point of the ruling: the
--     message now gives a reason that is true of every multi-clause group,
--     instead of a reason that was true of neither this program nor its twin.
GIVEN n  IS A NUMBER
      p2 IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE owedOn m Landlord IS 0
DECIDE owedOn n a        IS a's monthly_rent

-- 14. THREE CLAUSES, so the read sits TWO fall-throughs deep. The desugarer
--     nests `__pm_fallthrough_1` inside `__pm_fallthrough_0`, and the verdict
--     and the sentence must be the same at that depth as at the first — the
--     flag the message is written from is set by the innermost enclosing
--     synthesised binding, and a flag that only reached depth one would leave
--     the deeper read with a sentence that denies its own clauses.
GIVEN y IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE deepRent Landlord IS 0
DECIDE deepRent Agent    IS 1
DECIDE deepRent y        IS y's monthly_rent

-- 15. A SUB-PATTERN THAT IS A CONSTRUCTOR DECLARED IN A SECTION. `Flat Weekly`
--     matches only the `Flat`s billed weekly, so a `Flat Metered` reaches
--     clause 2 and clause 1 consumes nothing. The section gives `Weekly` a
--     second, qualified spelling, and while the irrefutability test compared
--     written names it did not recognise the unqualified one as a constructor:
--     it called this pattern an irrefutable binding, recorded the whole
--     constructor as consumed, and certified the read. (Kept last in the file
--     because a heading is open-ended.)
§ `Billing`

DECLARE Cadence IS ONE OF
    Weekly
    Metered

DECLARE Charge IS ONE OF
    Flat    HAS cadence IS A Cadence
    Usage   HAS unit_price IS A NUMBER

GIVEN c IS A Charge
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE priced (Flat Weekly) IS 0
DECIDE priced c             IS c's unit_price
